---
// How Kyle builds with AI coding agents. A short, human-facing summary of the
// principles; the full rules the agents follow live in github.com/kyle-pace/principles.
import DevLayout from 'kp-design/layouts/DevLayout.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const REPO_URL = 'https://github.com/kyle-pace/principles';
---

<DevLayout
  title="How I build"
  description="How a product manager ships apps and sites with AI coding agents, and the principles every project follows."
  image="/og-how-i-build.png"
  imageAlt="How I build: a product manager who ships software, with AI agents and a shared set of principles."
>
  <article class="page">
    <header class="top">
      <p class="path" aria-hidden="true"><span class="caret">~/</span>how-i-build</p>
      <h1>A product manager who ships software.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I’m Director of Product at <a href="https://www.localpublic.tv/">Local Public TV</a>. On nights and weekends I
        build apps and sites with AI coding agents, including Henwood, Seattle Grocery Deals and the experiments on this
        site. I don’t write the code by hand. This page is how I make that work, and the principles every project
        follows.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="row" aria-labelledby="who">
      <h2 id="who">Not quite a developer, not just a PM</h2>
      <div class="body">
        <p>
          I came to product by way of UX design and project management, not computer science. But I really am building
          apps now, and that’s an odd place to be.
        </p>
        <p>
          The way I make sense of it: coding agents moved the hard part. Typing code used to be the scarce skill. Now
          it’s deciding what to build, writing a clear brief, setting constraints, judging what comes back and saying
          “no, not like that.” That’s work I already do every day.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="row" aria-labelledby="how">
      <h2 id="how">How a project runs</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Decide what it’s for.</strong> Every project starts with a short brief: who it’s for, what it should
          do and what it shouldn’t.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Give it its own repo.</strong> One project, one repo, one deploy. Small pieces are easier to reason
          about, and easier to throw away.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Set the rules once.</strong> The principles below sync into every project, so whichever agent I use
          starts from the same standards.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Review everything.</strong> Every session starts fresh, and an agent sounds just as sure of itself
          when it’s wrong. So I review every result, ask it to explain choices I don’t follow, and try things myself
          before they ship. Less manager, more editor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Let checks catch what I miss.</strong> Every build runs an accessibility check and fails if a page
          loads anything from a server I haven’t approved.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ship small.</strong> Changes go out in small pieces, so I can actually check them.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="row" aria-labelledby="principles">
      <h2 id="principles">The principles</h2>
      <div class="body">
        <dl class="principles">
          <div id="accessibility">
            <dt>Accessibility first</dt>
            <dd>
              Designed in from the first commit, not audited in at the end. At least WCAG 2.2 AA, everything works
              with a keyboard, contrast holds up in light and dark, and layouts survive 200% text zoom and reduced
              motion.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div id="privacy">
            <dt>Privacy right behind</dt>
            <dd>
              Collect the least data that answers a real product question. No ad pixels, no fingerprinting, no analytics
              cookies, nothing personal in analytics, and no analytics at all if your browser sends Global Privacy
              Control or Do Not Track.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div id="screens">
            <dt>Every screen size from day one</dt>
            <dd>
              Phone, tablet and big screens are designed together, not stretched or squeezed in later. Apps support
              iPhone and iPad from the start.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div id="self-host">
            <dt>Self-host everything</dt>
            <dd>
              Libraries and fonts ship with the project, never from a CDN. Every outside service gets approved one at a
              time, per project, and an automated check fails the build if something sneaks in.{' '}
              <a href="/connections/">Here’s the full list.</a>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div id="correct">
            <dt>Correct and reliable</dt>
            <dd>
              Anything that reads outside data is tested against real samples, one bad record never takes down the rest,
              and a failing job fails loudly instead of publishing empty data.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div id="fast">
            <dt>Fast on a normal phone</dt>
            <dd>
              Pages have budgets: little JavaScript, small images, and the main content up within a couple of seconds on
              a mid-range phone.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div id="look">
            <dt>Its own look</dt>
            <dd>
              Each project gets a design taken from its own subject, not the generic defaults AI tools reach for. One
              bold idea, everything else quiet. Type is Atkinson Hyperlegible everywhere.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="more">
          <a href={REPO_URL}>The full rules the agents follow, on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="row" aria-labelledby="learning">
      <h2 id="learning">What I’m still learning</h2>
      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The gap isn’t syntax. It’s judging the output, because when I direct agents, I’m the only reviewer. So I keep
          working on understanding code well enough to judge it, and on data, which matters most for my day job right
          now.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</DevLayout>

<style>
  .top {
    max-width: 52rem;
  }

  .path {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .caret {
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }

  h1 {
    max-width: 16ch;
  }

  .lede {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .row {
    margin-top: var(--space-16);
  }

  .row h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .body > p + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* Steps: a numbered list, the number large and in the accent color. */
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: step;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .steps li {
    counter-increment: step;
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 2.5rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .steps li::before {
    content: counter(step);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: -0.2em;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }

  /* Each principle: a bold name, then a short paragraph, with a rule between them. */
  .principles {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .principles > div + div {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .principles dt {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .principles dd {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
  }

  .more {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* Wide screens: headings in a left column beside the text. */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 15rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 0 var(--space-12);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .row h2 {
      font-size: var(--step-1);
    }
  }
</style>
